<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Mountbatten</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lord Mountbatten, Earl Mountbatten of Burma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Mountbatten was a British naval officer who became the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a> in 1947. He brought forward the transfer of power and oversaw the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in August 1947. He stayed on as Governor-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of India until 1948, and was killed by an IRA bomb in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten.jpg" alt="Louis Mountbatten" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allan Warren · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lord_Mountbatten_Naval_in_colour_Allan_Warren_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
